+<h2>Model</h2>
+<ul>
+<li><code>AeroZipCompressor(input_dim=64, latent_dim=8)</code> - nn.Module returning (latent, reconstructed)</li>
+<li><code>compression_ratio(input_dim=64, latent_dim=8)</code> - returns input_dim / latent_dim</li>
+</ul>
+<table>
+<tr><th>encoder</th><td>Linear(input_dim, 32) -&gt; ReLU -&gt; Linear(32, latent_dim)</td></tr>
+<tr><th>decoder</th><td>Linear(latent_dim, 32) -&gt; ReLU -&gt; Linear(32, input_dim)</td></tr>
+<tr><th>forward</th><td>Returns both tensors: the latent is the product, the reconstruction is the loss signal</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Size</th><td>4,744 parameters at 64 -&gt; 8; 2,794 when the platform instantiates it on its 38-column feature table with latent_dim=4</td></tr>
+</table>
+<h2>Advanced concepts</h2>
+<p class="note">The ideas this model actually leans on, each stated with its failure mode.</p>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Rate-distortion, not just dimension</h3>
+<p>8:1 is a count of dimensions, not of bytes actually sent. A defensible bandwidth claim needs entropy-coded latent bitrates measured on real backhaul traces - the model reports compression_ratio() and nothing more.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Reconstruction error as a free detector</h3>
+<p>An autoencoder trained on healthy telemetry misfits when the plant changes. The same forward pass therefore yields two products: a latent for backhaul and a scalar residual that behaves like an unsupervised anomaly score.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Latent as transferable features</h3>
+<p>The bottleneck is reused as the input view for the tabular voters (m10, m11) and the SSL encoder, so a cheap linear layer at the edge can produce features the cloud classifiers already understand.</p>
+</div>
